Date: Sat, 12 Jun 93 10:43:19 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Contrapositive Crows

Dan Davis writes, in defense of his claim that my argument leads to the
conclusion that a white piece of paper provides evidence that crows are
white (via the chain "white piece of paper is evidence that everything
smaller than a buick is white, which implies that crows are white):

>I have just used your principle: if I see an X, and it is Blah, that
>provides evidence that all Xs are Blah. (call this principle *)
>
>X - Smaller than a buick
>Blah - White

...

>Thus "contrapositive crows" is an invalid argument based on
>invalid methodology. If you think it uses valid methodology, and in
>particular, that your evidence heuristic holds for any set, then you have to
>accept my buick argument. It uses exactly those principles. 

I seem to have been unsufficiently clear in explaining why your argument
does not follow from my principle. A white piece of paper is indeed
evidence for the proposition that everything smaller than a buick is white
(assuming we have not yet seen any non-white things smaller than a
buick--as I mentioned earlier in the thread, the argument I am using
depends on starting with a non-zero probability for the conclusion you are
trying to prove). But evidence that all things smaller than a buick are
white is not necessarily evidence that crows are white--"evidence for"
meaning, in both cases, something that increases the probability that the
proposition is true. To see why that is the case, consider the following
simple example:

Suppose there are only two classes of things smaller than a buick--crows
and pieces of paper. Further suppose, to make life very simple, that there
is only one of each in existence, so observing a white piece of paper not
only increases the probability that all pieces of paper is white, it
increases it to unity. Finally, suppose that the ex ante probability that
the crow is white is .5, that the piece of paper is white is .5, and the
two probabilities are independent.

Under these assumptions, the ex ante probability that all things smaller
than a buick are white is .5 x .5 = .25. 

We look at the piece of paper, and indeed it is white. That increases the
probability that all pieces of paper are white to 1, and increases the
probability that everything smaller than a buick is white to 1 x .5 = .5.

Note that, consistent with your (and my) principle *, observing an X
(something smaller than a buick--specifically, the piece of paper) that is
blah (white) is evidence here that all X's are Blah--our observation has
raised the probability that everything smaller than a buick is white. So my
particular example fits principle *.

Note also that the probability that all crows are white has not changed--in
my example it is still .5 . So, in this example, observing a sheet of white
paper is evidence for (increases the probability of) the proposition that
everything smaller than a buick is white, but does not increase the
probability that a crow is white. I think that shows why your conclusion
does not follow from my principle.

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


