Date: Sat, 12 Jun 93 11:00:47 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Against Nanotech As A Public Good

Tim Starr writes:

>The most disturbing thing that comes to my mind when Drexler argues for
>coercive support of nanotech as a public good is: how much?

...

>Take Drexler's claim that we lose 25 million dollars per day without
>nanotech per day at its face value.  That's 9 trillion, 125 million dollars
>lost per year.  Last I heard, the GDP of the USA was about 6 trillion.
>
>So, how much support should nanotech R&D get?  On the public goods argument,
>it would seem that it should get the ENTIRE GDP, plus another 3 trillion or
>so!  So, in addition to consuming all GDP, we'd have to consume capital, too,
>which would reduce GNP growth...  Where does it end?

This is simply wrong. Nothing in economics implies that the total amount
you should spend on producing something, whether a private good or a public
good, is equal to its value.

Suppose we were considering how expensive a car Tim should buy. Better (and
more expensive) cars are more valuable to him. Under his rule, we would
look for that quality of car for which total value was equal to total cost
(say $30,000 each). Buying that car would make him no better off than not
buying it. On the other hand, buying a $10,000 car that was worth $15,000
to him would make him $5,000 better off, and is obviously a more sensible
choice.

The correct rule is that you maximize net benefit by choosing the level of
quality for which total value minus total cost is at its maximum, which
implies that marginal value equals marginal cost (both on the margin of
increased quality).

Applied to nanotech (or any other research in basic science), and putting
aside all other problems associated with government subsidy of research,
that implies that you increase the budget as long as an additional dollar
of expenditure produces more than an additional dollar of benefit. To know
how large a budget that implies, you would need to know not only the benfit
per year of having nanotech but also the function relating how much is
spent on research to how soon we get nanotech.


David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


