Date: Sat, 12 Jun 93 16:39:05 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Ray Cromwell asks:

> Isn't [the Deutsch test of many-worlds] basically the same as the
> "quantum eraser" setup? 

Yup, Son of the Quantum Eraser meets AI.

> Measure a particle, "unmeasure" it and its original state is 
> restored. Frankly, I'm a little skeptical how far this information
> destruction/ state reconstruction process can be taken.

Provided the processes involved are reversible they can be taken as far
as we like.  Life, at the moment is an inherently irreversible process. 
Every thing we do increases entropy, causing the branching of worlds. 
When we have complete control over ourselves and our design (read:
nanotech) then the branching will not be such a well defined process.

At the moment people can claim the wavefunction collapse is triggered
by the intervention of an observer because our clumsy interventions
always introduce entropy somewhere along the measurement chain, which
has the effect of multiplying worlds (=> locally observed wavefunction
collapse).  Thus the predictions of Copenhagen are consistent with Many-
Worlds, in the low-tech world we live in.

At the level of design Deutsch is contemplating, in his EraserHead
computation, measurement is not associated with entropy release.  The
mental state of a machine has to be considered as a quantum
superposition to get the correct results (ie those in line with
experiment).  "Unmeasurements" are as easy to perform as "measurements",
I claim, in a reversible environment.

>   For instance, suppose my computer measures a particle in a trap, and
> then John peeks at the screen. John takes a vow of silence. Five years
> later, the computer tries to "unmeasure" the particle in the trap.
> Will it be restored to its original state, or will the process be
> spoiled by John's knowledge?

Spoiled by John's knowledge.

> What if I kill John first, then have the computer
> unmeasure the particle? 

Unmeasuring the particle would involve undoing _all_ the effects of the
broadcast of the test result (including wiping John's memory of the
event, at the very least).

> There's a lot of speculation in popular science
> books about macroscopic tests of quantum theory (macroscopic 
> matter wave interference, e.g. baseball double slit experiment) but
> I remain skeptical about them working.

Do you include the quantum optical experiments where excited atoms are
fired through double slit cavities?  I have an article about the
experiments (not theory) at hand.  They were touted as Quantum Eraser
experiments.

>. I guess my main argument is I
> don't see how a machine with finite memory store a quantum state. 
> Sure, it could store the result of a spin measurement, but how 
> can it reverse the interaction with the test electron?

Perhaps I misunderstand you, but Stern-Gerlach experiments are as easy
to run backwards as forwards.  You 'test' the spin of an electron by
passing it through a magnetic field, physically separating the different
spin components.  To reverse the interaction, feed the _correlated_
beams through another suitable field.  I don't see the problem with the
reversible memory of a machine being read in some analogous fashion.

> -- Ray Cromwell

