A few observations on nutrient synergy.  Taking Marty's words at face
value:

1	Zinc is needed for an enzyme that converts beta-carotene into
A in the gut and liver.  A zinc deficiency may impair absorption of A.
``In addition, the release of retinol [vitamin A] from the liver is a
zinc dependent process so a zinc deficit will cause a vitamin A
deficit even if your liver has plenty of vitamin A.''

2	C is needed for one of the enzymes that convert A (retinol)
into retinoic acid, which has multiple immune benefits, the functions
which are given below in the post.  So taking A or beta-carotene
without C doesn't allow one the full benefit of A.

I emailed Marty, and he has written his own nutrition textbook.  I
didn't get the name of the publisher, and I also do not know who to
trust when it comes to nutrition.

Trying to understand the ins and outs of the nutrition/supplement game
is particularly annoying as varying sources will contradict one
another, even accuse others of outright lies.  I can second Tim May's
recommendation of Michael Colgan's _Optimum Sports Nutrition_, as it
seems to have more integrity than most, and there is a wealth of
information contained within.  Also, for anybody who has read Pearson
and Shaw's _Life Extension_, you really should also read the followup
_Life Extension Companion_ (not certain if that is the exact title).
It has slightly more up to date information, and also reprinted an
interesting article listing many of the carcinogens found in the food
we eat, including particular vegetables, grains, fruits.

I am sorely looking for trustworthy and informative sources regarding
nutrition and supplements.  Too many sources are too layman-oriented.
I want to know biochemical enzymatic mechanisms, not *just* the
overall physiological or psychopharmacological effects.

