Patton was one of the best Tank Commanders for the U.S. during World War 
II, but Patton had a very bad habit of out running his supply lines.
We have nutrient reserves for a purpose.  During infectious stress(viral, 
bacterial or parasitic) we are put into a hypermetabolic mode, we loose our 
appetite and digestion from the gut is impaired.  We use our bodies 
nutrient reserves to fight the infection.  If the reserve is low we don't 
fight so good(just like Patton when his tanks ran out of gas).  If your 
liver doesn't have much vitamin A, you will have to increase your dietary 
intake of vitamin A.  Kids have low reserves for both iron and vitamin A.
Since pathogens can intercept our nitrients as they are being absorbed from 
the gut(especially iron and zinc), you are always better off not 
supplementing during and infection(except for vitamin C) and relying on 
your bodies nutrient reserves to fight the good fight.  But if you are 
caught off guard and you don't have good reserves, you will have to 
supplement and hope the enemy doesn't get too much of what you are trying 
to send to the front line troops(white blood cells).


Almost any clinical chemistry lab can run a serum retinol.  But you will 
not need to have one run if you follow my advise and not exceed 25,000IU of 
retinol per day(10,000IU per day if you are a woman who could become 
pregnant).  Get the rest of your vitamin A from Beta-carotene.  If you are 
not in a hurry to add retinol to your liver, go mostly with beta-carotene.
In addition to being a precursor for vitamin A(retinol), it's a pretty good 
antioxidant.

