Speaking of which, do Extropians have any particularly Extropian music
recommendations?  Even if one is perhaps ``vegging'' by listening to
music, I certainly feel there are some forms of music which may not be
capital-E Extropian, but can certainly be described as more
``extropianly'' than others.

I am a classical music fan, particularly of Beethoven, Brahms,
Sibelius.  If I had to choose, the two works most meaningful to me
would have to be Beethoven's 9th symphony and Brahms' 1st piano
concerto (both works are in D minor; and one of my favorite works of
Bach is the Toccata and Fugue in D minor; funny how this is).
Overall, I prefer the more symphonic pieces, particularly the more
dramatic ones of Beethoven,the Romantic composers, and a few twentieth
century works.

When it comes to classical music, the matter of the conductor matters
enormously -- a good conductor can make a piece, and a bad conductor
can break it.  Which orchestra is playing generally matters less than
who is conducting.

There has been list discussion of favorite Extropian science fiction
authors, and music -- certain types of music -- can communicate a
gestalt nonverbal Extropianism as directly as any other medium, if not
even more so.  People often speak of affecting their consciousness and
emotions, and I have found that putting on a good piece of music can
be potent mind/mood medicine at the right times.

-Han Y. Huang                                    hhuang@athena.mit.edu

