I listen to lots of different kinds of music, including the classical stuff
you like, and rock, and jazz, and folk, and world music (I'm a sucker for
anything with polyrhythms in it).  But *extropian* music...hmm...

What feels most extropian to me is the part of my collection that I think of
as "serious guitar-head music".  Elaborate instrumental electric guitar with
a mix of rock, blues, jazz and speed-metal influences; Joe Satriani, Jeff Beck,
Eric Johnson, Steve Vai, Pat Bonilla, Steve Morse, Yngve Malmstein, Al DiMeola,
John McLaughlin.  Groups like Brand X, the Dixie Dregs, the Mahavishnu
Orchestra.

I find a mix of energy and intelligence there that's unmatched anywhere else.
Joe Satriani on "Surfing with the Alien" screaming through solos as elaborate
as Bach fugues at relativistic velocity --- ahhhh, now *that's* extropian!

Not for the faint-hearted, though.  This stuff is brain music meant to be
played loud.  It also helps a lot to be a musician yourself, as I am.  A
lot of non-musicians have trouble processing this stuff, which is why it's
always been a minority taste.

Pulling CDs off the shelf behind me at random ---

"Flying In A Blue Dream" Joe Satriani (Relativity 1989)
     Satriani's third.  Great stuff, especially the title track and "The
Mystical Potato-Head Groove Thing", "One Big Rush" and "Back to Shalla-Bal".
Album as a whole slightly marred by Satriani's attempts at singing.

"Ominous Guitarists from the Unknown" (Shrapnel Records 1991)
     An anthology of piece by 10 little-known guitarists.  Cutting-edge stuff,
lots of speed-metal influence, some of it rather spiky and difficult at first
hearing.  My personal favorite is Craig Erickson's "South Train Blue".

"Ah Via Musicom" Eric Johnson (Capitol 1990)
    Another guitarist-singer, but with a much better voice than Satriani.
His playing style shows Texas-blues influence and is relatively sweet and
accessible --- a good introduction to the joys of hot guitar if your ears
aren't already tuned for it.

"Guitar Shop" Jeff Beck (CBS Records 1989)
    The man who virtually *invented* ambitious instrumental rock guitar with
his 1974 album "Blow by Blow" (highly recommended) demonstrates that he's
still got it fifteen years later.  Don't miss the hilarious parody voiceover
in the title track.

"Surfing With The Alien" Joe Satriani (Relativity 1987)
    The essential Satriani, an absolute must-have for any fan of guitar-head
music.  The central section of "Crushing Day" is one of the finest three
minutes in the history of the electric guitar.  Also not to be missed: the
incredible title track, "Circles", and "Lords of Karma".

"Still Life (Talking)" Pat Metheny (Geffen 1987)
    A jazzier, less muscular approach from Pat Metheny.  Metheny is generally
considered the brightest star in jazz guitar today, and upholds this
reputation with an album of meditative, piercingly beautiful music.  And no
feedback :-).

"Blow By Blow" Jeff Beck (Epic 1974)
    This album created the tradition all these others are operating within.
It's twenty years old now but hasn't dated a day.  Mainstream music *still*
hasn't caught up to what Beck and Hammer were doing on "Blue Wind".  "Since
We've Ended Now As Lovers" is still the classic study of violin-like tambre
control with feedback.  Everything else on the album has stood the test of
time just as well, and it's a must-have for any guitar-head.

"Unorthodox Behavior" Brand X (Charisma 1976)
    Jazzy, difficult stuff from a legendary studio session including Phil
Collins back when he was a real musician.  "Nuclear Burn", "Unorthodox
Behavior" and "Running of Three" are high points.

"Night Of The Living Dregs" Dixie Dregs (Polygram 1979)
    The one Dregs album to have if you only have one --- a classic.  Features
Steve Morse's electrifying "Punk Sandwich", but don't miss "Long Slow
Distance".  The first side is studio, the second recorded live at the Montreux
Jazz Festival.  Morse's droll, hyperkinetic takes on bluegrass and Texas swing
music give this album a humorous bite.

Guess I've rambled on enough.  But give this stuff a try --- it makes great
hacking music.
